{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q010_rossmann_family_function_divergence","answer":"SCOPe places alcohol-dehydrogenase-like C-terminal domains, CoA-binding domains, and potassium-channel NAD-binding domains in distinct families within the NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold domain superfamily (c.2.1), in fold c.2 and the alpha/beta class (c). This means SCOPe infers probable common ancestry despite substantial divergence. Sharing only the fold would establish structural similarity without necessarily establishing homology; sharing the class is much broader still.

Their relationship illustrates diversification of a nucleotide-binding scaffold, not equivalent biological function. Alcohol-dehydrogenase-like C-terminal domains typically provide the NAD(P)-binding portion of an enzyme whose catalytic machinery also involves another domain. CoA-binding domains accommodate a different nucleotide-containing cofactor and participate in different biochemical contexts. Potassium-channel NAD-binding domains occur in potassium-channel or transport-associated regulatory contexts; their classification does not imply alcohol-dehydrogenase activity or that the domain itself forms an ion-conducting pore. The superfamily name does not mean every member binds NAD(P).

Consequently, neither substrate specificity, NAD versus NADP preference, CoA binding, catalytic activity, nor a particular channel-regulatory mechanism should be transferred across these families from the shared classification alone. Such assignments require sequence and binding-site comparisons, ligand-bound structures where available, and biochemical or physiological evidence. A cross-family structure may provide a template for the conserved core, but binding loops, ligand recognition, and domain interfaces require separate validation. For comparative sampling, include representatives of all three families rather than treating one superfamily representative as functionally representative of all three.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
